SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF
ECHO === PART 2 of 3 -- a pending journal, and the table HELD by another owner ===
ECHO
ECHO A two-record committed journal sits beside JRN5 and a foreign LIVE table
ECHO lock is in place. USE fires recover_table_buffer_journal.
ECHO
ECHO   SAFE      -> 0 applied, 2 not placed, THE LOG IS KEPT, and USE says so
ECHO                plainly. Nothing is lost; the rows wait for the lock to go.
ECHO   URGENT    -> the log is deleted, or USE claims a recovery. Then the
ECHO                whole-loop fence is not a nicety and must be built now.
ECHO   ALSO READ -> whether USE opens the table at all. If recovery refusing
ECHO                also refuses the OPEN, that is its own finding.
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
USE JRN5
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10
USE
ECHO === part 2 done ===
